#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198521 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aaa6b152-86d3-5f96-8a94-57f86a17a84d" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Flux Over a Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aaad3095-de33-5bcb-be0b-11e3e89e5d1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194028} {:answer/id #uuid "5ba4ad0b-e2ea-57da-a46a-c216574e6e58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194029} {:answer/id #uuid "6285ef5f-358a-5af2-9d99-fc50a255471c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194030} {:answer/id #uuid "6e21e94d-351c-5b88-9f81-8d69600a53b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194031} {:answer/id #uuid "7c408b1f-d464-509d-9cfa-a3e8b47733e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194032}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ba4ad0b-e2ea-57da-a46a-c216574e6e58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194029} :answer-field/id #uuid "e73670ae-5b02-5574-9ae0-660067f313d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194027}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dd29749c-bfb5-5726-8ec7-04f9f714fe37" :question/math-academy-id "q-160888" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨z+y,\\,2y+z,\\,xy⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z=x-y$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x-y)=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x-y)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨z+y,\\,2y+z,\\,xy⟩{|}_{z=x-y} \\\\  & =⟨(x-y)+y,\\,2y+(x-y),\\,xy⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{{x}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{x+y}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{xy}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨z+y,\\,2y+z,\\,xy⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(x)\\cdot 1-(x+y)\\cdot (-1)+xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-x+x+y+xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(y+xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(y+xy)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}x{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2+2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[2x+{x}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(2(1)+{1}^{2})-0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
